<title>UKRAINE: Canada grants $7.5 million to help shut Chernobyl.</title>
<headline>Canada grants $7.5 million to help shut Chernobyl.</headline>
<dateline>KIEV 1996-10-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>Canada's Foreign Minister on Thursday pledged $7.5 million to help close Ukraine's Chernobyl power plant, site of the world's worst nuclear accident, and vowed to back the former Soviet republic in the West.</p>
<p>Lloyd Axworthy said Canada, home to a big ethnic Ukrainian community, would speak for Kiev in the Group of Seven rich nations which have promised $3 billion to close Chernobyl.</p>
<p>&quot;The projects include radiation monitoring and development of a special licensing system,&quot; he told a news conference.</p>
<p>The funding would also help modernise Ukraine's ageing and inefficient hydroelectric plants, he added.</p>
<p>Ukraine has complained about a delay in the arrival of G7 cash to help shut Chernobyl, whose fourth reactor exploded 10 years ago and sent a radiation cloud across much of Europe.</p>
